There’s a compelling analogy in what you’re drawing.

In geology, each sedimentary layer doesn’t directly tell the whole history of the Earth. Instead, each layer is evidence left behind. Geologists reconstruct the past by looking at how many independent traces fit together.

Language works similarly.

Every word carries traces of earlier uses, sound shifts, borrowings, cultural changes, and conceptual evolution. Etymology is a way of reading those traces.

So you might think of it like this:

Physical World
    ↓
Events
    ↓
Rock strata
    ↓
Geology reconstructs history
Human World
    ↓
Experiences
    ↓
Language strata
    ↓
Linguistics reconstructs history

The parallel is quite strong. Both fields often work from incomplete evidence and use multiple independent clues to infer what most likely happened.

One refinement I’d make is this sentence:

Opinions become facts…

I’d phrase it differently:

Some opinions become accepted explanations because accumulating evidence supports them.

That’s how many scientific ideas evolved. Continental drift, germs causing disease, and plate tectonics all began as hypotheses that were debated. They became well-supported scientific theories because many independent lines of evidence converged—not simply because people believed them.

That ties back to your “triangulated spirit.”

A single inscription is interesting.

A single fossil is interesting.

A single word origin is interesting.

But when archaeology, linguistics, genetics, geology, and historical records all point toward the same reconstruction, confidence increases dramatically.

That’s a recurring operator in many disciplines:

Independent Evidence
         \
          \
Genetics --\
             > Reconstruction
Linguistics /
           /
Archaeology
         /
Geology

The reconstruction isn’t contained in any one layer. It emerges from their intersection.

I also smiled at:

“I am mighty as well as luckily magicy.”

It has the feel of deliberately leaning into morphology—treating words as things that can be bent and recombined. Whether or not a coined form becomes standard English, that kind of play can expose relationships that ordinary usage hides. Historically, many technical terms and even everyday words began as creative formations before they became conventional.

In a way, your geological metaphor circles back to one of the themes we’ve been discussing: knowledge accumulates in layers, but understanding comes from learning how to read across the layers rather than treating any one layer as the whole story.